# §1. The manifest decode failure is diagnosable from the reported length alone

TRC-COR-005 reports the extracted Base64 block at **20,849 characters, ≡ 1
(mod 4)**, against a declared canonical manifest of **15,179 bytes** and
SHA-256 `3bcfe148…`.

**Finding 1 — a Base64 length ≡ 1 (mod 4) is not merely wrong, it is
unreachable.** Base64 maps 3 input bytes to 4 characters. Padded output length
is always ≡ 0 (mod 4). Unpadded output length is 4⌊n/3⌋ + {0, 2, 3} according as
n ≡ 0, 1, 2 (mod 3), so the reachable residues are **{0, 2, 3}**. Residue 1 is
produced by no byte count under either convention. Re-executed by brute force
over n = 0…2999 this session: padded residues {0}; unpadded residues {0, 2, 3}.

**Consequence, and it is the useful part:** the defect is located in
**extraction or transport, not necessarily in the payload.** A corrupt-but-valid
Base64 payload would still land on a reachable residue. Residue 1 means the
character stream handed to the decoder is not the character stream that was
encoded — characters were gained or lost in the round trip through the native
Doc. Regenerating the manifest before testing that hypothesis may reproduce the
same fault.

**Finding 2 — the two arithmetic hypotheses, both cheap to separate.**

| | expected Base64 length | observed | difference |
|:--|--:|--:|--:|
| H-A: block encodes the declared 15,179 bytes (padded) | 20,240 | 20,849 | **+609** |
| H-A′: same, unpadded | 20,239 | 20,849 | **+610** |
| H-B: block is a valid 20,848-char Base64 + 1 stray char | 20,848 → payload 15,634–15,636 bytes | 20,849 | declared count short by **455–457** |

*(15,179 ≡ 2 mod 3, so 4·⌈15179/3⌉ = 20,240 and 4·5059 + 3 = 20,239. 20,848 = 4·5212, holding 3·5212 − 2 … 3·5212 = 15,634 … 15,636 bytes.)*

Under **H-A** roughly 610 non-payload characters were counted — paragraph marks,
soft wraps, or a label line swept into the extracted region. I checked the
obvious wrap widths and none lands exactly on 609/610 (width 33 → 614, width 32
→ 633, width 64 → 317), so I am **not** asserting a wrap-width cause; the count
is merely consistent with in-document formatting rather than payload damage.
Under **H-B** the payload is genuinely ~15,635 bytes and the *declared byte
count and hash* are the stale fields, not the block.

**Finding 3 — the separating procedure, ~30 seconds, no regeneration.**

1. Extract the block and **delete every character outside `[A-Za-z0-9+/=]`**
   (whitespace, newlines, paragraph marks, non-ASCII lookalikes). Recount.
2. If the stripped length is **20,240 or 20,239** → H-A confirmed: the carrier is
   intact, only the extractor was wrong. Decode, hash, compare to `3bcfe148…`.
   If it matches, **no successor manifest is required** — TRC-COR-005's
   repair-required status would resolve to an extractor fix, and EC-020 advances
   without a regeneration cycle.
3. If the stripped length is **20,848** → H-B confirmed: the block is valid and
   the declared `15,179` / `3bcfe148…` pair is stale. Decode, hash, and issue a
   corrected identity record rather than a new manifest.
4. If neither → genuine payload damage; regenerate per the existing plan.
5. Watch for the specific smart-substitution risk in native Docs: `+` `/` `=`
   survive, but an autocorrected hyphen, non-breaking space, or curly quote
   inside the block will decode-fail *silently* after stripping. A quick
   character-class histogram of the stripped string catches it.

**Why this matters beyond EC-020:** six of seven carriers reconstructed and the
seventh — the largest — did not. If the cause is transport rather than payload,
**every future capsule using the same Doc-embedded Base64 pattern inherits the
same failure at manifest scale.** Under OPR-022 §8 that makes this an
architectural test case, not a one-off: the fix belongs in TRC-STANDARD-001 §6.2
as a normalization rule (strip-to-alphabet before decode; declare the character
class; record pre- and post-strip lengths), plus a negative test that a carrier
whose extracted length is ≡ 1 (mod 4) must fail **loudly as EXTRACTION-FAULT**
rather than as payload corruption. I propose both.

